The overall asymptotic complexity of the uniqueness check algorithm is determined by the most expensive phase. Sorting has a complexity of $ O(n \log n) $, and the scanning phase has a complexity of $ O(n) $. Since $ O(n \log n) $ grows faster than $ O(n) $ for large $ n $, the sorting phase dominates.

Thus, the **governing asymptotic cost of the algorithm is $ O(n \log n) $**.

Example:
```c
int unique3(int a[], int n) {
  sort(a, n); // O(n log n)
  for(int i = 1; i < n; i++ ) {
    if(a[i-1] == a[i]) return 0; // O(n)
  }
  return 1;
}
```

Modify the nested loop to check each pair only once by ensuring `j` starts from `i + 1` and removing the `i != j` condition:

```c
int unique1(int a[], int n) {
  for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
      if(a[i] == a[j]) return 0;
  return 1;
}
```

To enforce child-before-parent ordering in a recursive tree traversal using OpenMP tasks, insert a `#pragma omp taskwait` directive before the `process(p)` call. This ensures that all tasks created for the left and right children complete before the parent node is processed. The minimal directive insertion is as follows:

```c
void traverse(struct node *p) {
    if (p->left)
        #pragma omp task
        traverse(p->left);
    if (p->right)
        #pragma omp task
        traverse(p->right);
    #pragma omp taskwait
    process(p);
}
```

Here is a minimal code fragment that wraps a tree traversal task in a `taskgroup` directive, ensuring synchronization and checking before moving to the next step, while allowing a background task to run in parallel:

```c
#pragma omp parallel
#pragma omp single
{
  #pragma omp task
    start_background_work();

  for (int i = 0; i < max_steps; i++) {
    #pragma omp taskgroup
    {
      #pragma omp task
        compute_tree(tree); // recursively creates tasks
    }
    check_step(); // executed after the taskgroup completes
  }
}
```

This ensures that the background task is not included in the synchronization of the tree traversal tasks.

