To safely abort an NCCL communicator during initialization if a timeout occurs or an error is detected, you must first initialize the communicator in **nonblocking mode** using the `ncclConfig_t` structure and set `config.blocking = 0`. This allows calls to `ncclCommAbort` at any time without getting stuck in NCCL operations.

Then, during initialization, repeatedly check the communicator's status using `ncclCommGetAsyncError`. If a timeout occurs (determined via a user-defined `checkTimeout` function) or an error is detected (`state != ncclSuccess`), you set an abort flag. After synchronizing this flag across all ranks (e.g., via a `reportErrorGlobally` function), call `ncclCommAbort` on the communicator.

Here's a relevant code snippet:

```c
bool globalFlag;
bool abortFlag = false;
ncclConfig_t config = NCCL_CONFIG_INITIALIZER;
config.blocking = 0; // set communicator as nonblocking
CHECK(ncclCommInitRankConfig(&comm, nRanks, id, myRank, &config));

do {
  CHECK(ncclCommGetAsyncError(comm, &state));
} while(state == ncclInProgress && checkTimeout() != true);

if (checkTimeout() == true || state != ncclSuccess) abortFlag = true;

/* synchronize abortFlag across ranks */
reportErrorGlobally(abortFlag, &globalFlag);

if (globalFlag) {
  ncclCommAbort(comm);
  restartNCCL(&comm); // user-defined function
}
```

This ensures that all ranks safely abort the communicator if a timeout or error is detected during initialization.

