A `SplitEnumerator` can be implemented to periodically discover splits and assign them to `SourceReader`s by using the `callAsync()` method of the `SplitEnumeratorContext`. This allows the `SplitEnumerator` to perform active coordination without maintaining its own threads. The method schedules a function to be called repeatedly at a fixed interval, and upon each invocation, it discovers new splits and assigns them to the `SourceReader`s.

Here is an example in Java:

```java
class MySplitEnumerator implements SplitEnumerator<MySplit, MyCheckpoint> {
    private final long DISCOVER_INTERVAL = 60_000L; // 60 seconds
    private final SplitEnumeratorContext<MySplit> enumContext;

    MySplitEnumerator(SplitEnumeratorContext<MySplit> enumContext) {
        this.enumContext = enumContext;
    }

    private List<MySplit> discoverSplits() {
        // Implementation to discover splits
        // This is a placeholder for the actual discovery logic
        return new ArrayList<>();
    }

    @Override
    public void start() {
        enumContext.callAsync(this::discoverSplits, (splits, thrown) -> {
            Map<Integer, List<MySplit>> assignments = new HashMap<>();
            int parallelism = enumContext.currentParallelism();
            for (MySplit split : splits) {
                int owner = split.splitId().hashCode() % parallelism;
                assignments.computeIfAbsent(owner, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(split);
            }
            enumContext.assignSplits(new SplitsAssignment<>(assignments));
        }, 0L, DISCOVER_INTERVAL);
    }

    // Other required methods for SplitEnumerator implementation go here
}
```

In this example, the `start()` method schedules the `discoverSplits()` method to be called every 60 seconds. When splits are discovered, they are assigned to the `SourceReader`s based on the current parallelism.

