ORCv2 emulates dynamic linking by applying the same symbol resolution and linking rules used in static and dynamic linking during JIT compilation. It organizes code into separate `JITDylib` instances (e.g., `LibA`, `LibB`), each representing a logical module. When a `JITDylib` is created, it defines symbols, and link order determines symbol resolution priority. Symbols from `LibA` are preferred over `LibB` if both define the same symbol and `LibA` is linked first.

Here’s an example of creating and linking separate JITDylib modules:

```c++
ExecutionSession ES;
RTDyldObjectLinkingLayer ObjLinkingLayer(
    ES, []() { return std::make_unique<SectionMemoryManager>(); });
CXXCompileLayer CXXLayer(ES, ObjLinkingLayer);

// Create JITDylib "A"
auto &LibA = ES.createJITDylib("A");
CXXLayer.add(LibA, MemoryBuffer::getFile("a1.cpp"));
CXXLayer.add(LibA, MemoryBuffer::getFile("a2.cpp"));

// Create JITDylib "B"
auto &LibB = ES.createJITDylib("B");
CXXLayer.add(LibB, MemoryBuffer::getFile("b1.cpp"));
CXXLayer.add(LibB, MemoryBuffer::getFile("b2.cpp"));

// Create main JITDylib and set link order
auto &MainJD = ES.createJITDylib("main");
MainJD.addToLinkOrder(&LibA);
MainJD.addToLinkOrder(&LibB);
CXXLayer.add(MainJD, MemoryBuffer::getFile("main.cpp"));
```

When resolving symbols (e.g., `main`), ORCv2 respects the link order and ensures that duplicate definitions within the same JITDylib cause errors, while separate JITDylibs can define the same symbol without conflict.

